# Normalise line endings. Every .md file in the previous version of this
# repository was committed with CRLF endings, which makes diffs noisy and
# breaks shebangs and heredocs when the same tree is checked out in CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Extensionless executables that MUST be LF. `* text=auto eol=lf` above already
# covers these, but stating them explicitly is worth the two lines: a CRLF in the
# shebang makes the hook fail with `bash\r: No such file or directory`, an error
# that names neither the cause nor the file, and which only appears on Windows.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
justfile text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary -- never diff or normalise these.
*.onnx binary
*.pt binary
*.pth binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
# Compressed and array formats. `text=auto` usually detects these, but a
# gzipped CSV is exactly the kind of file that gets mangled by line-ending
# normalisation if detection guesses wrong.
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.bz2 binary
*.xz binary
*.npy binary
*.npz binary
# Raw little-endian float32 blobs -- the golden parity fixtures. `text=auto`
# would almost certainly detect these as binary on its own, because float data
# is full of NUL bytes. Almost certainly is not a guarantee, and a fixture
# quietly corrupted by CRLF translation would make the Rust parity test fail
# for a reason nobody would think to look for.
*.f32 binary
*.parquet binary
*.sqlite binary

# Generated / vendored files: keep them out of language statistics and
# collapse them in pull-request diffs.
Cargo.lock linguist-generated=true
pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated=true
web/src/lib/vendor/** linguist-vendored=true

# Diagram sources are text but are not meaningfully reviewable as diffs.
*.mdj -diff
*.puml text
*.dbml text
